= Peanut (Groundnut, 花生)

_Scope: this entry treats *Arachis hypogaea* L. (Fabaceae) — the seed, the oil, the thin red seed-coat (a drug in its own right in Chinese medicine), and *peanut butter* as its principal modern preparation. *The peanut is not a nut*: it is a legume, and a geocarpic one — after fertilization the stalk bends down and drives the developing pod *underground* to ripen, which is why half the world's names for it mean "earth-something." Status: *post-Columbian everywhere outside South America*, and the entry marks that plainly. Domesticated in the southern Bolivia–northern Argentina region and carried worldwide by Portuguese and Spanish shipping from the sixteenth century, it has no entry in the Shennong Bencao Jing, no Sanskrit name, and no place in the Greco-Arabic canon. It does have a *genuine but late* Chinese drug register, entering the supplementary Bencao literature by the Qing; Ayurveda and Tibb sections are reasoned extension, flagged. Three things make it consequential out of proportion to its classical standing: the *Atlantic return route* that carried it to Africa and back to North America with a Bantu name; *aflatoxin*, for which it is among the highest-risk crops in the food supply; and *peanut allergy*, where a single trial reversed a generation of medical advice._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* *_Arachis hypogaea_ L.*, family Fabaceae (Faboideae) — a *legume*, related to beans, peas and lentils, not to the tree nuts it is grouped with in cookery and in allergy labelling. *The binomial names the plant's strangest habit:* *_Arachis_* from Greek *ἄρακος (árakos)*, a leguminous plant of Theophrastus; *_hypogaea_* from *ὑπόγαιος (hypógaios,* "under the earth") — for *geocarpy*, the ripening of the pod underground. The flower is borne above ground and self-pollinates; the fertilized ovary is then pushed down on an elongating stalk, the *peg* or gynophore, until it buries itself and the pod matures in darkness. Almost no other crop does this, and the fact organizes the plant's names worldwide.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott (s.v. ἄρακος, ὑπόγαιος); Linnaeus, _Species Plantarum_ (1753). Geocarpy and the gynophore ("peg") mechanism: standard agronomic and botanical references. Domestication in the southern Bolivia / northwestern Argentina region, with early archaeological material from coastal Peru, is the standard account in crop-origin literature.]

*The world's names for the peanut fall into two families, and the split is itself informative.*

*(1) Named for where the pod grows — underground:*
- *English* *groundnut*; *Latin* *hypogaea*; *Chinese* *落花生 (luòhuāshēng)*, "fallen-flower produce" — the name is a *botanical observation*, recording that the flower drops and the fruit forms below; commonly shortened to *花生 (huāshēng)*. Also *長生果 (chángshēngguǒ,* "longevity fruit"), a folk-auspicious name.
- *Nahuatl* *tlālcacahuatl* — *tlālli* "earth" + *cacahuatl* "cacao bean," i.e. *"earth cacao"* — which passed into Mexican Spanish as *cacahuate* and into French as *cacahuète*. The Aztecs named it by analogy to the cacao bean it resembled.
- *Persian* *بادام زمینی (bādām-e zamīnī)*, "earth almond"; *Hindi* *मूंगफली (mūṅgphalī)*, "mung-fruit," naming it by analogy to the familiar pulse.

*(2) Named for where it came from — Africa, in the Old World's reckoning:*
- *Arabic* *فول سوداني (fūl sūdānī)*, "Sudanese bean." The Arabic name records the peanut arriving *from Africa*, not from the Americas — a two-stage transmission preserved in the word.

*The Atlantic return route, and the word* goober. The peanut travelled South America → West Africa (with Portuguese shipping, from the sixteenth century) → North America, the last leg in the hands of *enslaved West and Central Africans*, who carried both the crop and its names. *Goober*, still current in the American South, is from Kikongo/Kimbundu *nguba*; *pindar*, the older Carolina term, from Kikongo *mpinda*. These are among the clearest surviving Bantu loanwords in American English, and the peanut's North American career begins not with European agronomy but with African foodways. *The crop that Americans think of as native to the South arrived there by way of Angola and the Congo.*#footnote[_Goober_ < Kikongo/Kimbundu _nguba_ and _pindar_ < Kikongo _mpinda_: _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "goober", "pindar"); standard works on Africanisms in American English (Turner, _Africanisms in the Gullah Dialect_). The South America → West Africa → North America transmission is the standard account in crop-history literature. Nahuatl _tlālcacahuatl_: standard Nahuatl lexicography; the derivation of Spanish _cacahuate_ and French _cacahuète_ from it is uncontested.]

*Portuguese (Brazilian):* *amendoim*, from Tupi *mandubi / manobi* — an indigenous South American name surviving in Portuguese, appropriate for a crop domesticated on that continent. *Spanish (South America):* *maní*, from Taíno — the Caribbean indigenous name, and the usual word across most of Spanish-speaking South America, as against Mexican *cacahuate*.

*Chinese:* *落花生 / 花生 (luòhuāshēng / huāshēng)*; the red seed-coat is a separate drug, *花生衣 (huāshēng yī,* "peanut coat") or *花生紅衣*. *Introduction and attestation, flagged:* the peanut reached China in the *sixteenth century* via Portuguese and Spanish trade routes (the Fujian and Guangdong coast, and via Manila). It is treated in the Qing supplementary pharmacopoeial literature — notably *本草綱目拾遺 (Běncǎo Gāngmù Shíyí,* Zhao Xuemin, 1765), the supplement to Li Shizhen's compendium, whose very purpose was to record drugs the 1578 original lacked. *Whether 落花生 appears in the 1578 本草綱目 itself is complicated by name-ambiguity* — the characters are used in some earlier texts for other plants — and the question should be checked against a specific edition rather than settled from secondary sources. What is secure: the peanut is a *late, post-Columbian* entrant to Chinese materia medica, and nothing in the Han canon concerns it.

*Japanese:* *落花生 (rakkasei)*; *ピーナッツ (pīnattsu)*. *Korean:* *땅콩 (ttangkong)*, "earth bean" — another underground-naming.

*Sanskrit:* *no classical name.* The peanut is post-Columbian in India; the modern *मूंगफली (mūṅgphalī)* is a vernacular analogy-coinage, not a dravyaguna lemma. See the Ayurveda section.

*English:* *peanut* (pea + nut, recorded from the eighteenth century), *groundnut*, *goober*, *pindar*, *earthnut*, *monkey nut*. *Peanut butter* is a late-nineteenth-century American product name; its history is treated under Formulas.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Late but genuine — not a reasoned extension. The peanut has a real place in Chinese dietetics and in the supplementary Bencao literature from the Qing onward, and a *distinct drug-form* in the seed-coat. It is simply not classical in the Han-canonical sense, and the entry does not pretend otherwise._

*Property (性 xìng):* Neutral (平).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Lung (肺).

*Actions and indications:*
- *健脾養胃 (jiàn pí yǎng wèi)* — strengthens the Spleen and nourishes the Stomach: the governing dietetic action, for poor appetite and thin, weak constitutions. The sweet-neutral-nourishing profile places it with the gentle food-tonics rather than with any potent drug.
- *潤肺化痰 (rùn fèi huà tán)* — moistens the Lung and transforms phlegm: for *dry* cough, and chronic cough in the depleted — the Lung-channel action. Peanuts stewed with rock sugar, or peanut-and-pear preparations, belong here.
- *通乳 / 下乳 (tōng rǔ / xià rǔ)* — promotes lactation. *花生豬腳湯* — peanuts simmered with pig's trotters — is one of the standard Chinese postpartum galactagogue dishes, in the same dietetic register as the crucian-carp soup noted in `tilapia.typ`. A living folk-dietetic practice rather than a textual prescription.
- *止血 (zhǐ xuè)* — stops bleeding: *and this action belongs specifically to the seed-coat*, not the kernel. *花生衣 (huāshēng yī)*, the thin red testa, is used for bleeding of vacuity type and has a genuine modern Chinese clinical register in *thrombocytopenia* and thrombocytopenic purpura, where preparations of the coat are given to raise platelet counts. *This is the one part of the peanut that functions as a drug rather than a food*, and it is also the part discarded by every industrial process — blanched peanuts and most peanut butter have no testa at all.

*Cautions:* the kernel is rich and oily, and classical dietetic sources caution it in *Spleen vacuity with damp* and in loose stools or diarrhoea, where the oiliness is poorly handled; likewise in patients with sluggish digestion or gallbladder disease. Raw and mouldy peanuts are warned against in the folk register — a caution that modern aflatoxin science has vindicated emphatically.#footnote[花生 and 花生衣 in the Qing supplementary Bencao literature, notably Zhao Xuemin, _Běncǎo Gāngmù Shíyí_ (本草綱目拾遺), 1765; modern Chinese materia medica and dietetics references for properties, channels and the 花生衣 thrombocytopenia use. The 花生豬腳湯 lactation dish is living folk dietetics rather than a textual formula — flagged. The 花生衣 platelet application is a modern Chinese clinical register; evidence quality should be checked against current literature before being relied on.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Arachis hypogaea _is post-Columbian in India, with no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus and no Sanskrit name — the modern_ mūṅgphalī _is a vernacular coinage by analogy with mung. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other post-Columbian entries (`acai.typ`, `guarana.typ`, `amaranth.typ`). The irony worth noting: groundnut oil is today one of the principal cooking oils of western and southern India, and peanut is ubiquitous in Indian food, so a substance with no classical standing whatever has become dietetically central within about two centuries._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), with a faint Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent) note from the testa.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Guru (गुरु, heavy — markedly so), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous — the defining quality, at ~49% fat).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) in the mild degree — consistent with the pan-Indian folk reading of peanuts as a *winter* food, and with the common complaint that they provoke heat and acne in excess. *Flagged as reasoned*, though the Chinese neutral (平) attribution is the better-grounded of the two readings and the divergence is slight.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Prabhāva:* *None* — and the honest statement is that *none is warranted*. The peanut's actions follow directly and unremarkably from madhura rasa, guru-snigdha guṇa and madhura vipāka: a heavy, oily, sweet seed nourishes, builds tissue, and burdens weak digestion. Nothing about it is unpredictable from its parameters, which is precisely the criterion for *declining* to assign a prabhāva (Caraka Sū. 26.67–72). *If* a candidate were forced, it would be the seed-coat's haemostatic action — but that belongs to a different part of the plant and to a different tradition.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* *Vāta-śāmaka* (guru-snigdha-madhura is the canonical Vāta-pacifying combination); *Kapha-vardhaka* (heavy, oily, sweet — the classic Kapha-aggravating triad); *Pitta-vardhaka* in excess, via the oiliness and the mild uṣṇa note.

*Reasoned karma:* _Balya_ (strengthening), _bṛṃhaṇa_ (nourishing, anabolic — the governing action for a dense oilseed), _vṛṣya_ (generative-tissue-promoting, as for oilseeds generally), _stanya-janana_ (lactation-promoting — converging with the Chinese 通乳 use, which is a real cross-system agreement reached independently), _snehana_ (internally oleating). Burdensome to _agni_ in quantity; *āma*-producing where digestion is weak.#footnote[Reasoned placement only; no classical Ayurvedic entry exists for a post-Columbian American legume. The convergence between the reasoned _stanya-janana_ reading and the Chinese 通乳 dietetic use is noted as independent agreement, not as shared transmission. Prabhāva declined explicitly per Caraka Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72 and this book's Prabhāva rule.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha — post-Columbian substance; extensions of conditional rules plus the modern tier, which for the peanut is unusually important; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* _Agni-viruddha_ where digestion is weak — a heavy oilseed in a _manda-agni_ patient produces _āma_; _doṣa-viruddha_ in Kapha excess and in damp conditions; _mātrā-viruddha_ is the practical one, since peanuts are eaten by the handful rather than the spoonful.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* the Indian folk reading of peanuts as heating and acne-provoking; the Chinese caution against raw and mouldy nuts.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological — three items, and the first two are serious:*
  - *Peanut allergy.* One of the most clinically significant food allergies: IgE-mediated, to the storage proteins *Ara h 1, 2, 3* and *6*, with *anaphylaxis* a real risk at trace exposure in sensitized individuals. Prevalence rose markedly in Western countries through the late twentieth century. *For an allergic individual this is an absolute contraindication*, and no part of this entry's dietetic discussion applies to them.
  - *Aflatoxin.* Peanuts are among the *highest-risk crops in the human food supply* for contamination by *Aspergillus flavus* and *A. parasiticus*, which produce *aflatoxins* — potent *hepatocarcinogens*. The risk is one of *growing, drying and storage conditions*, not of the species as such: warm humid storage is what produces it. In regions with poor post-harvest infrastructure the dietary burden is substantial, and *aflatoxin exposure interacts synergistically with chronic hepatitis B* to raise hepatocellular carcinoma risk far above either factor alone. Regulated limits exist in most jurisdictions. *This is the single most important safety fact about the peanut* and is treated further below.
  - *Oxalate and purines* — modest; of limited practical significance against the two above.
  - *Commercial peanut butter additives* — hydrogenated oils, added sugar and salt in many products; a formulation issue rather than a property of the food.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Arachis hypogaea _is absent from the Avicennan canon and from Ibn al-Bayṭār's_ al-Jāmiʿ_: a South American legume that reached the Old World only after those works were written. The modern Arabic name *فول سوداني (fūl sūdānī,* "Sudanese bean") is itself the evidence — it records arrival from Africa in the modern period, and no classical Arabic drug-name exists. Modern Unani practice in South Asia treats it, where at all, as an ordinary nutritive food. The placement below is reasoned extension on the humoral axis._ See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Names:* Arabic *فول سوداني (fūl sūdānī)*; Egyptian colloquial also *سوداني (sūdānī)*; Persian *بادام زمینی (bādām-e zamīnī,* "earth almond"); Urdu *مونگ پھلی*.

*Reasoned mizāj:* *Hot and Moist, 1st degree* — the standard placement for oily nutritive seeds and nuts in the Tibb framework, alongside almond (*lawz*) and walnut (*jawz*), to which a Tibb physician meeting the peanut would most naturally assimilate it. The Persian name *"earth almond"* makes exactly that assimilation.

*Reasoned afʿāl (actions):* *Mughadhdhī* (nourishing — the governing action); *muqawwī al-jasad* (body-strengthening); *muraṭṭib* (moistening); *mulayyin* in a mild degree; *muḥarrik al-bāh* (aphrodisiac) by the class convention attaching to oily nuts; *mudirr li-l-laban* (galactagogue), converging with the Chinese and reasoned-Ayurvedic readings.

*Reasoned istemālāt:* *ḍuʿf* (debility) and convalescence; thinness and insufficient nourishment; dry constitutions.

*Reasoned mawāneʿ:* phlegmatic (*balghamī*) constitutions and conditions of excess moisture, in which a Hot-Moist oily food is contrary; weak digestion. The modern allergy and aflatoxin cautions have no classical correlate and are flagged as modern.

*Reasoned muṣliḥ / badal:* oily nuts are conventionally corrected with honey, or taken with a warming aromatic to assist digestion; plausible class-level *badal* within the actual pharmacopoeia would be *lawz* (almond) or *fustuq* (pistachio). *Explicitly reasoned, not textual.*

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none, and none possible* — the plant was unavailable in the seventh-century Ḥijāz and in the Old World entirely. Flagged because the modern ubiquity of peanuts across the Muslim world could otherwise invite a spurious pedigree.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *花生豬腳湯* — peanuts simmered with pig's trotters; the standard postpartum lactation dish (通乳), and the clearest instance of the peanut's dietetic rather than pharmacological role.
- *花生衣 (huāshēng yī)* — the red seed-coat, prepared as decoction or in modern tablet/extract form for bleeding and low platelet counts; *the one genuinely drug-like use of the plant*.
- *花生紅棗湯* — peanuts with jujube, a blood-and-qi-supporting household preparation (see `jujube-dates.typ`).
- *Peanuts stewed with rock sugar* for dry cough (潤肺).
- *醋泡花生* — vinegar-steeped peanuts, a *modern* Chinese folk practice for blood pressure and cholesterol; post-classical and evidentially unsupported, flagged as folk.
- *花生油 (huāshēngyóu)* — peanut oil, a principal cooking oil of southern China.

=== Ayurveda

*No classical formula* — post-Columbian, non-canonical. Modern Indian use is nonetheless extensive and worth distinguishing from classical standing: *groundnut oil (mūṅgphalī kā tel)* is a major cooking oil of Gujarat, Maharashtra and the south; *chikki* (peanut-and-jaggery brittle — see `jaggery.typ`) is a national confection; peanuts are a standard *vrata* (fasting-day) food, permitted where grains are not, which has given them a quasi-religious dietary niche acquired entirely in the modern period.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*No Greco-Roman or classical European tradition* — the plant was unknown to the Old World before the sixteenth century, and the Greek *árakos* behind the genus name denotes a different legume entirely.

*The substantive Western register is modern, American, and industrial — the peanut butter story:*
- *Invention.* Ground peanut paste has indigenous South American and West African precedent, but *peanut butter as a manufactured product* is late-nineteenth-century North American. *Marcellus Gilmore Edson* (Canada) patented a peanut paste process in *1884*; *John Harvey Kellogg* patented a process in *1895* and promoted it as a sanitarium protein for patients who could not chew; *Ambrose Straub* patented a grinding mill in *1903*. Commercial stabilization by *partial hydrogenation* in the 1920s–30s (the Rosefield process behind the familiar national brands) is what made it a shelf-stable staple rather than a separating paste.
- *A persistent misattribution, flagged.* *George Washington Carver did not invent peanut butter.* He promoted peanut cultivation as a soil-restoring rotation crop for Southern farmers depleted by cotton and devastated by the boll weevil, and published long lists of peanut uses — genuinely important agricultural work — but the product predates his involvement and the credit is popularly misassigned. Correcting this does not diminish Carver; it describes what he actually did.
- *Other Western preparations:* roasted and salted nuts; *boiled peanuts* (green peanuts boiled in brine — a Southern US and Chinese preparation, quite unlike the roasted nut); peanut oil as a high-smoke-point frying fat; *peanut flour*.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*No classical preparation* — see above. Modern Middle Eastern and North African consumption (roasted *sūdānī*, peanut-studded confections, *ḥalāwa* variants) is contemporary.

*West African note, recorded because it is the peanut's other great culinary tradition:* the groundnut stew of the Sahel and West Africa — Senegambian *maafe / mafé*, and the groundnut soups of Ghana and Nigeria — is a major cuisine built on the crop during its post-Columbian naturalization there, and it is the tradition from which the American *goober* register descends. Culinary and economic rather than medical, and noted as such.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The peanut is a *warm-season annual of sandy, well-drained soils*, needing roughly four to five frost-free months and, critically, *dry conditions at harvest and in storage* — a requirement that is not merely agronomic but the central food-safety fact about the crop. As a legume it *fixes nitrogen*, which is why it recurs across the world as a rotation crop on exhausted soils. See `climates.typ`.

=== Hot-humid and subtropical South America (the origin zone)

The domestication region of southern Bolivia and northwestern Argentina, with early archaeological material from the arid Peruvian coast — where the dryness that preserves archaeological peanuts is the same dryness that keeps stored ones safe. *Brazilian register:* *amendoim* is woven through the national sweet tradition — *paçoca* (crushed peanut, cassava flour and sugar, pressed into a crumbly block) and *pé-de-moleque* (peanut brittle) are the canonical *Festa Junina* confections of the June harvest festivals, and peanuts enter Bahian cooking in *vatapá* and *caruru* alongside dendê palm oil and dried shrimp (see `fish.typ` for the Bahian moqueca register).

=== Sahelian and West African (the naturalized zone that became an economy)

Senegal, Gambia, Nigeria, Mali: the peanut naturalized so thoroughly after Portuguese introduction that it became a staple and then, under colonial administration, a *monoculture export* — the "groundnut economy" of Senegambia, with the attendant soil and food-security costs of growing a cash crop in place of food. Culinarily, the *maafe* groundnut-stew tradition. *Climatically the Sahel suits the plant well in the growing season but poorly in storage*: hot humid post-harvest conditions are exactly what produce aflatoxin, and the dietary aflatoxin burden in parts of West Africa is among the world's highest.

=== Warm-temperate and subtropical East and South Asia (the largest production)

China is the world's largest producer; India follows. The Chinese register is *oil crop plus snack plus dietetic food* (花生油, boiled and roasted nuts, the lactation and Lung-moistening dishes above); the Indian register is *oil crop plus confection plus fasting food*. Both are post-Columbian adoptions that became dietary mainstays within a few centuries.

=== Warm-temperate North America (the return leg)

The American South, where the crop arrived with enslaved Africans, was promoted as a soil-restoring rotation after the boll weevil, and was industrialized into peanut butter. *Boiled peanuts* in the South East preserve a preparation closer to the African and Chinese green-peanut registers than to the roasted-and-ground product.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Two transpositions, of different kinds. The first is *formulation*: a dense, nutritious oilseed has been reformulated into a product frequently carrying *added sugar, salt and hydrogenated fat*, eaten in quantities no traditional register contemplated — a _mātrā_- and _saṃskāra_-order shift rather than a geographic one, and the reason "peanut butter" and "peanuts" are not nutritionally interchangeable. The second is *storage climate*: the crop moves freely between climates, but *the safety of the crop does not travel with it* — peanuts grown or stored in hot humid conditions without drying infrastructure carry an aflatoxin burden that peanuts from a regulated supply chain do not, and this is the rare case where the climate of a food's *handling* matters more to the eater than the climate of its growing. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, raw peanuts, approximate per 100 g — verify against USDA FoodData Central.

- Energy ~567 kcal; Water ~6 g
- *Protein ~26 g* — exceptionally high for a plant food; limiting in methionine and lysine, so complementary with cereals
- *Fat ~49 g* — chiefly *monounsaturated* (oleic) and *polyunsaturated* (linoleic); low in saturated fat
- Carbohydrate ~16 g; *Dietary fibre ~8.5 g*
- *Niacin ~12 mg* (very high), *Vitamin E ~8 mg*, folate, thiamine, *biotin*
- Magnesium ~168 mg, phosphorus ~376 mg, potassium ~705 mg, manganese, copper, zinc
- *Arginine* — peanuts are among the richest common dietary sources
- *Resveratrol*, phytosterols, *p*-coumaric acid; the *testa* carries most of the proanthocyanidins

*Peanut butter, approximate per 100 g:* ~588 kcal, protein ~25 g, fat ~50 g. *The nutritionally consequential variable is the ingredient list*, not the peanut: products range from 100% ground peanuts to formulations with added sugar, salt and hydrogenated vegetable oils. Compare labels rather than assuming equivalence.

*Pharmacology and clinical points of note:*
- *Cardiovascular.* Large prospective cohorts associate regular nut consumption — *including peanuts, which behave nutritionally like tree nuts despite being legumes* — with lower cardiovascular and all-cause mortality; peanuts specifically are well represented in these datasets, which matters because they are far cheaper than tree nuts and so carry the association into populations where almonds and walnuts do not reach. *Observational; confounding by overall diet quality is the standing caveat.*
- *Glycaemic and satiety effects* — low glycaemic index, high satiety per calorie; peanut consumption is not associated with weight gain in cohort data despite the energy density.
- *Aflatoxin — the dominant safety consideration.* Produced by *Aspergillus flavus* and *A. parasiticus* in warm, humid storage; *aflatoxin B1 is a Group 1 human carcinogen* (IARC), acting on the liver, and its interaction with *chronic hepatitis B infection* is *multiplicative* rather than additive for hepatocellular carcinoma risk. Regulatory limits apply in most jurisdictions (the EU and US set limits in the low parts-per-billion for foods intended for direct consumption; verify current figures). *Practical implications:* discard shrivelled, discoloured or mouldy nuts; the risk concentrates in informally traded and poorly stored supply, not in regulated commercial product; and the classical Chinese caution against mouldy peanuts turns out to have been pointing at a real carcinogen centuries before it was characterized.
- *Peanut allergy, and the finding that reversed the guidance.* Through the 1990s and 2000s, guidance in several countries advised *delaying* peanut introduction in infancy to prevent allergy. The *LEAP trial* (Learning Early About Peanut Allergy, 2015) randomized high-risk infants to early peanut introduction at 4–11 months versus avoidance and found *early introduction reduced the development of peanut allergy by roughly 80%*. Guidance was revised accordingly in the US, UK and elsewhere. *This is one of the clearest instances in modern nutrition of a plausible precautionary recommendation turning out to have been actively harmful*, and it is worth stating in those terms. Oral immunotherapy (a standardized peanut-protein product was approved in the US in 2020) offers desensitization, not cure, for established allergy.
- *Resveratrol* — present, at lower levels than in grapes or wine; the popular health claims attached to it substantially outrun the evidence (cf. `raisins.typ`).#footnote[Composition: USDA FoodData Central — verify. Nut consumption and cardiovascular/all-cause mortality: large prospective cohorts (Nurses' Health Study, Health Professionals Follow-up Study) and the PREDIMED trial for the Mediterranean-diet-with-nuts arm; observational associations with the usual confounding caveat. Aflatoxin B1 as IARC Group 1 carcinogen and its multiplicative interaction with hepatitis B in hepatocellular carcinoma: IARC monographs and the hepatology literature; regulatory limits vary by jurisdiction and require current verification. LEAP trial: Du Toit et al., _New England Journal of Medicine_, 2015, and the subsequent LEAP-On follow-up; guidance revisions followed in the US (NIAID addendum, 2017) and elsewhere — verify current national guidance. Peanut oral immunotherapy: US approval 2020.]

*Safe daily intake / upper limit:* no toxicological ceiling as a food for non-allergic individuals; the limits are *contamination* and *allergy*, not the nut.
- *Typical beneficial intake in cohort studies:* roughly *28–42 g/day* (a small handful) of nuts including peanuts.
- *Absolute contraindication:* diagnosed peanut allergy — trace exposure can be life-threatening.
- *Infants:* current guidance in several countries *recommends early introduction* (around 4–6 months, in an age-appropriate form — never whole nuts, which are a choking hazard) for allergy prevention, particularly in infants with eczema or egg allergy; this reverses older advice and should be followed per *current national guidance and, in high-risk infants, with medical supervision*.
- *Aflatoxin:* prefer regulated commercial supply; discard mouldy or discoloured nuts; store dry and cool.
- *Peanut butter:* read the ingredient list; the food and the formulated product differ.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice* — peanut allergy in particular is a matter for clinical management.

